@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex overview
@cindex passkey
@cindex FIDO2

Passkeys are WebAuthn credentials: key pairs that a web site (a
@dfn{relying party}) registers instead of, or in addition to, a
password.  They are kept by an @dfn{authenticator}, usually a USB
security key.  gkey is an authenticator implemented in software on
the computer itself, which keeps the private keys inside the
computer's Trusted Platform Module (TPM).

gkey consists of two programs:

@table @command
@item gkey-service
A system service, running under a dedicated account, that creates a
virtual USB security key through the kernel's UHID driver and answers
the requests that web browsers send to it, on behalf of the user of
the active session.  It speaks the Client to Authenticator Protocol,
version 2.0 (CTAP2), over the CTAPHID transport, so any browser that
supports FIDO2 security keys can use it without further support.

@item gkey
A command line tool with which each user lists and deletes their own
passkeys.  It talks to @command{gkey-service} over the D-Bus system
bus.
@end table

gkey supports discoverable credentials (``passkeys'' proper, that let
you sign in without typing a user name) as well as server-side
credentials, and the ES256 signature algorithm.  Every operation is
confirmed by the user through the desktop's polkit authentication
dialog.

@node Security model
@chapter Security model
@cindex security
@cindex TPM
@cindex root of trust

Only the @samp{gkey} account, which @command{gkey-service} runs as,
may use the TPM and create HID devices.  Users get neither right, and
the service identifies, for each request, the user it acts for: the
user of the active session for requests from the browser, the caller
for requests made with @command{gkey}.

All keys descend from a @dfn{storage primary key} that the TPM
derives, each time gkey connects to it, from the secret seed of its
owner hierarchy.  The seed never leaves the TPM, and nothing is stored
in the TPM's own non-volatile memory.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Each user has a @dfn{user key}, a storage key created by the TPM under
the primary key and protected by a random 256-bit password.  The TPM
refuses to load anything below a user key without its password, which
only the service can read.  Even other programs allowed to use the
TPM, such as other members of the @samp{tss} group, therefore cannot
use the passkeys of any user.

@item
Each credential has its own ECC P-256 signing key, generated by the
TPM under the user key.  The private part is returned only in a form
encrypted by the TPM (``wrapped''), and the key is created with the
@code{fixedTPM} and @code{fixedParent} attributes: it can be used on
this TPM only, and cannot be exported or duplicated.  Signing takes
place inside the TPM.

@item
The list of a user's credentials (relying parties, user names, wrapped
keys, signature counters) is encrypted and authenticated with
AES-256-GCM.  The 256-bit key is generated by the TPM and @dfn{sealed}
under the user key.  The keystores live in the service's state
directory, which users cannot read or replace, so they can neither
tamper with them nor roll them back to an earlier state.

@item
Commands are sent to the TPM in a session salted with the primary key,
with parameter encryption, so that passwords and the sealed keys do
not cross the bus between the processor and the TPM in the clear.

@item
Before a passkey is created or used, polkit asks the user for their
password, through PAM, in a dialog shown by the desktop
(@pxref{User consent}).  The service never sees the password.
@end itemize

gkey cannot protect against an attacker with root privileges, nor
against a compromise of the @samp{gkey} account, which can read the
passwords of the user keys.  A program running as the user can still
send requests to the security key, but each request needs the user to
authenticate in a dialog naming the web site.

Clearing the TPM (for instance from the firmware setup) changes the
owner seed and makes every stored passkey permanently unusable, as
does losing the state directory, which should be backed up; keep
another way to sign in to important accounts.

@node Setting up
@chapter Setting up
@cindex setting up
@cindex permissions

@samp{make install} installs a systemd unit, an account definition for
systemd-sysusers, a udev rule, a polkit policy, a D-Bus policy and a
list of kernel modules to load.  Then, as root:

@example
systemd-sysusers                # create the 'gkey' account
modprobe uhid
udevadm control --reload
udevadm trigger /dev/uhid       # apply the udev rule now
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable --now gkey.service
@end example

The service runs as the @samp{gkey} account, with the @samp{tss}
group that most distributions use for @file{/dev/tpmrm0}; the TPM2
Software Stack packages create it.  Do not add people to the
@samp{gkey} or @samp{tss} groups: gkey does not need it, and it would
let their programs create arbitrary HID devices or use the TPM.

Users need a polkit authentication agent in their desktop session, as
GNOME, KDE Plasma and most other desktops provide.  Browsers then
offer gkey like any other security key.

@node Invoking gkey-service
@chapter Invoking gkey-service
@cindex invoking @command{gkey-service}
@pindex gkey-service

@example
gkey-service [@var{option}]@dots{}
@end example

@command{gkey-service} creates the virtual security key and serves it
until it receives @code{SIGTERM} or @code{SIGINT}.  It is normally
started by systemd.

@table @option
@item -s @var{dir}
@itemx --state-dir=@var{dir}
Keep the keystores of the users in @var{dir}, in a subdirectory named
after each user ID (@pxref{Files}).  Without this option the first
directory of the @env{STATE_DIRECTORY} environment variable, which
systemd sets, is used, and otherwise @file{/var/lib/gkey} (more
precisely @file{@var{localstatedir}/lib/gkey}, as given to
@command{configure}).

@item -t @var{conf}
@itemx --tcti=@var{conf}
Reach the TPM through the TCTI (TPM Command Transmission Interface)
described by @var{conf}, such as @samp{device:/dev/tpmrm0} (the
default) or @samp{swtpm:host=localhost,port=2321} for a software TPM.
Without this option the environment variables @env{GKEY_TCTI},
@env{TPM2TOOLS_TCTI} and @env{TCTI} are consulted, in that order.

@item --uhid=@var{file}
Create the device through @var{file} instead of @file{/dev/uhid}.

@item --seat=@var{seat}
Serve the user of the active session of @var{seat}, by default
@samp{seat0}.  Only that user can open the device the service
creates.

@item --user=@var{uid}
Serve the user @var{uid} instead, without consulting
systemd-logind.  This is meant for testing.  When combined with
@option{--verify-command}, the service does not use the system bus at
all, and the management interface is not available.

@item --verify=@var{method}
Obtain user consent with @var{method}, @samp{polkit} (the default) or
@samp{command}.  @xref{User consent}.

@item --verify-command=@var{command}
Run @var{command} to obtain user consent.  Implies
@option{--verify=command}.

@item -v
@itemx --verbose
Print informational messages; give twice for debugging messages and
three times to trace every HID message.  The @env{GKEY_LOG}
environment variable accepts finer grained settings, in the syntax of
the Rust @code{env_logger} library.

@item -q
@itemx --quiet
Print only error messages.

@item --help
Display a summary of the options and exit.

@item --version
Display version information and exit.
@end table

The exit status is zero when the service stops because of a signal,
and non-zero when it fails.

@node Invoking gkey
@chapter Invoking gkey
@cindex invoking @command{gkey}
@pindex gkey

@example
gkey [@var{option}]@dots{} [@var{command} [@var{argument}]@dots{}]
@end example

@command{gkey} manages the passkeys of the user running it, through
@command{gkey-service}, which must be running.

@table @code
@item list
List your credentials: their ID, relying party, user, kind
(@samp{passkey} for discoverable credentials, @samp{server-side}
otherwise), creation date, and the number of times they were used.
This is the default command.

@item delete @var{id}@dots{}
Delete your credentials whose ID starts with each @var{id}.  Each
@var{id} must designate exactly one credential.  polkit asks you to
authenticate first; over SSH, run @command{pkttyagent} to answer in
the terminal.  The relying party is not told; remove the passkey from
your account settings too.

@item info
Describe the TPM, your keystore and the authenticator.
@end table

@command{gkey} accepts the options @option{--help} and
@option{--version}.  The exit status is zero on success and one on
failure.

There is no command to export credentials: their private keys cannot
leave the TPM.

@node User consent
@chapter User consent
@cindex user presence
@cindex user verification
@cindex polkit
@cindex PAM

FIDO distinguishes @dfn{user presence}, the user's consent to an
operation, from @dfn{user verification}, which also establishes who
the user is.  Relying parties ask for one or the other.

With the default @samp{polkit} method, both are obtained by asking
polkit to authorize the action
@samp{io.github.krishap_s.gkey.use-passkey} for the session of the
user.  The authentication agent of the session shows a dialog naming
the web site and asks for the user's password; polkit checks it
through PAM, so any PAM module configured for polkit, such as a
fingerprint reader, can be used.  polkit offers no confirmation that
does not authenticate, so the user authenticates for every operation.
The policy installed by gkey asks every time and never remembers an
authorization.  Deleting passkeys with @command{gkey} requires the
action @samp{io.github.krishap_s.gkey.manage}.  Administrators can
change either rule with polkit rules files.

With the @samp{command} method, the command given with
@option{--verify-command} is run by @file{/bin/sh}, with
@env{GKEY_REQUEST} set to @samp{presence} or @samp{verification},
@env{GKEY_DESCRIPTION} set to a description of the request and
@env{GKEY_UID} to the user ID of the user.  Exit status zero grants
the request.

The request for consent is withdrawn when the browser cancels it.

@node Files
@chapter Files
@cindex files
@cindex keystore

@table @file
@item /var/lib/gkey/@var{uid}/keystore
The keystore of the user @var{uid}, readable only by the @samp{gkey}
account.  It holds the user key and its password, the sealed
encryption key, and the encrypted credential list.  Back up
@file{/var/lib/gkey}: the keystores are useless without the TPM, but
the passkeys are lost without them.

@item /var/lib/gkey/@var{uid}/keystore.lock
Lock file used to serialize access to the keystore.

@item /usr/lib/systemd/system/gkey.service
The systemd unit.

@item /usr/lib/sysusers.d/gkey.conf
Definition of the @samp{gkey} account.

@item /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-gkey.rules
Grants the @samp{gkey} group access to @file{/dev/uhid}.

@item /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/io.github.krishap_s.gkey.policy
The polkit actions.

@item /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/io.github.krishap_s.Gkey1.conf
Lets the @samp{gkey} account own the service's name on the system
bus, and everyone call it.
@end table

The management interface, @samp{io.github.krishap_s.Gkey1} at
@samp{/io/github/krishap_s/Gkey1}, offers the methods
@code{ListCredentials}, @code{DeleteCredentials} and @code{Info}.
They always act on the credentials of the calling user.

